MS-Windows even as of WinXP is still so badly broken
that it won't accept the same spelling for file
pathnames in commands that it allows in the file
system.

For an example, my comic linkmarks directory sits in
the WinXP filesystem as:

c:\documents and settings\xanthian\my documents\my pictures
\comiclinkmarks

which must instead be typed into a command line as
this hideous mess:

c:\docume~1\xanthian\mydocu~1\mypict~1\comicl~1

That kind of nonsense is simply unacceptable, and
causes brain damage when used.

It's _possible_ to work from the base command.com as
you describe, just hugely painful.  It's dozensfold
easier to just put a copy of tiny program
command.com right into the bones file directory, so
that when I open it, the long ugly file pathname is
already accomplished and now is spelled ".".

        [I probably have a dozen or more copies of
        command.com other places I need and want it
        to open with "." as some "chosen by me"
        local directory, too, NetHack isn't an
        isolated case.]

And you can't do the sane things you can in well
designed operating systems to get around this bad
design; more bad design blocks that. Put a shortcut
in the bones file directory to "the one and only"
command.com, click it, and command.com opens with
its default local directory, somewhere else, as ".",
not the directory where the shortcut sits.

Sigh. What Alexis said is the way to go; Hearse is
well able to open an MS-DOS window for itself, so if
I'd known to enable the "pause" as Alexis indicated,
I wouldn't have needed my local copy of command.com
at all to make reading Hearse's results possible. I
tried what he said, and it works, nice and slick.

